  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 202 PM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms \nwith very heavy rainfall will continue be possible the rest of today \nand Tuesday, and could result in an isolated flooding threat.\n\nThe upper pattern will continue to support a deep, moist southerly \nflow across central NC as an open trough currently aligned along the \nspine of the Appalachians closes off into an upper low over the \nSoutheast tonight, where it is forecast to linger through at least \nTuesday night. At the surface, a stationary boundary remains draped \nalong and just east of the Appalachians. This boundary, combined \nwith persistent low-level convergence between the trough and \nAtlantic ridge, will provide a focus for scattered showers and \nthunderstorms through the next 36 hours, with periods of heavy \nrainfall and isolated instances of flash flooding. The greatest \ncoverage of heavy rainfall may occur Tuesday morning into Tuesday \nafternoon as the upper low drifts across GA and induces a surface \nwave that lifts northward across central NC. Deep southerly flow off \nthe Atlantic will maintain abundant moisture, allowing storms to \nproduce efficient rainfall rates. While widespread flooding is not \nanticipated, localized excessive rainfall amounts will be possible \nwhere storms repeatedly move over the same locations, resulting in \nan increased risk of isolated flash flooding. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Diurnally driven showers and thunderstorms expected \nto continue through the seven day period.\n\nA broad upper trough will be across the eastern United States for \nthe second half of the week into the weekend. Between the upper \ntrough and a surface high over the western Atlantic Ocean, this will \ncontinue to pump moist southerly air into the region, and the chance \nfor showers and thunderstorms will continue to varying degrees \nthrough the extended forecast. Coverage will be the greatest on \nWednesday, with slightly less coverage into the beginning of next \nweek. While counties across the south should climb into the lower \n90s for the weekend, the rest of the area should have highs \nremaining in the 80s for the next several days.",
